               <code>
                  <coding>
                     <system value="http://loinc.org"/>
                     <code value="8648-8"/>
                     <display value="Hospital course note"/>
                  </coding>
               </code>
               <text>
                  <status value="generated"/>
                  <div xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">Dear colleague, I am writing to inform you about the hospital course of your 30-year-old male patient, admitted on 02/06/2006 with a two-day history of progressive right lower quadrant pain, nausea, and low-grade fever. Clinical examination revealed rebound tenderness at McBurney's point. Laboratory workup confirmed leukocytosis, and abdominal ultrasound supported the diagnosis of acute appendicitis. The patient was resuscitated with intravenous fluids and commenced on prophylactic antibiotics. Given the clinical picture, he underwent laparoscopic emergency appendectomy on 02/06/2006 without intraoperative complications. Histopathology confirmed acute appendicitis. Postoperatively, recovery was uneventful, vital signs remained stable, and wound care was performed regularly. Pain was managed adequately. The patient was mobilised early and discharged on 04/06/2006 in good general condition. Outpatient follow-up and wound inspection are recommended in 7–10 days.</div>
               </text>
            </section>
